CORRECTION — "lossy e7 is 2.54x cjxl, the worst ratio on our ladder (e3 is
0.71x, e9 1.06x)" is NOT reproduced anywhere in this grid, and it is the premise
the whole e7 attribution thread started from. At threads=1 e7 is 0.86-0.88x at
EVERY size, i.e. we are consistently faster than cjxl there, and e7 is the best
band we have at t=1. Whatever produced 2.54x was a different corpus, size,
thread count or build; do not carry that number forward.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY SLOW, in priority order:

1. **e3 at >= 1 MP, single-threaded: 1.40x (1024) / 1.45x (2048).** The worst
   band in the grid. It is NOT fixed overhead -- at 64 and 256 e3 sits at
   0.88-1.01x and only opens up as the image grows, so this is per-pixel cost.
   That makes it the top wall target.
2. **Thread scaling at e5: 1.32x (1024 t8) / 1.47x (2048 t8)** where the same
   cells are 0.91x / 0.93x at t=1. We convert threads into speed worse than
   cjxl does at e5 specifically -- e7 t8 is 0.99-1.08x and e9 t8 is 0.45-0.50x,
   so it is not a general parallelism deficit.
3. e9 t=1 is at parity (1.03-1.05x) while e9 t=8 is 0.45-0.50x: our e9 scales
   much better than cjxl's.

BYTES — at 64x64 we are 9-22 % LARGER than cjxl (1.151 at e3, 1.220 at e5,
1.204 at e7, 1.090 at e9) while at 256 and above we are at or below it.

  **CORRECTED 2026-09-10 by small_image_byte_gap_2026-09-10.{tsv,meta}** — this
  paragraph originally called that a "per-file intercept" that is
  "un-attributed", and two of those claims are wrong. The header+TOC part is a
  constant +4 B (colour) / +1..0 B (gray) and IS attributed: `--strategy libjxl`
  reproduces cjxl's header exactly, so it is the T4 `header_all_default_fast_paths`
  gate, Libjxl-only by design. The rest is section payload and does NOT behave
  like an intercept — webshot is +22 % at 64^2 and still +12.7 % at 256^2. The
  real shape is e3-e7 losses on graphics content at every size, with e9 favouring
  us in 7 of 8 (image, size) pairs. Read the 64x64 row as a sample of that, not
  as a small-image phenomenon.

CAVEATS: 4 images, 2 of them at 2048, so the 2048 byte ratios (0.652 at e5,
0.694 at e9) are two-image medians dominated by the brochure's patches win and
must not be read as a corpus figure. Distances are only {1.0, 4.0}; this is a
WALL measurement, and no quality metric was computed, so no RD claim follows
from it.
